#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221141 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2e37d37-007c-569b-a44c-cb6a9d075371" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "669126b0-2995-549c-9a80-888bccd3acc5" :question/math-academy-id "e-21845" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $10$-periodic extension of the function\n$F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$\ndefined on $[MATH: [-5,5).]$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $10$-periodic extension of $G(x)=5x$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$.\n\n> The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n> $XXXXX$\n> Since $f$ is $XXXXXXXX$\n> and $XXXXXXXX$\n> on $\\mathbb{R}$, we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n> $XXXXXX$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=5$. Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{107}{6} + \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{250(-1)^n}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{5}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that\n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-5,5)$,\n- continuous on $[MATH: [-5,5),]$ and\n- satisfies $F(-5)=F(5)$, if we extend the value at $x=5$ using the formula $F(x)=\\dfrac{5}{2}x^2-3$.\n\nTherefore, the $10$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{continuous}}$ and $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise smooth}}$\non $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{5}{2}x^{2} - 3) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{107}{6}) + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)\\frac{d}{dx}((250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼0 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(250(-1)^{n})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot (-\\frac{nπ}{5}\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5})) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(-50(-1)^{n})/(nπ)\\sin (\\frac{nπx}{5}) \\\\ 5x & ∼∑_(n = 1)^(∞)50 (-1) n + 1 n π \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 5)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221142 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/id #uuid "6e45e60d-7c68-5c49-8ca2-5381af39cd47" :question/math-academy-id "e-21846" :question/problem "$[MATH: {-2x, \\mid x ∈ [- 2, 0); x^{2}, \\mid x ∈ [0, 2)]$\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $[MATH: x \\in [-2,2).]$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in\\mathbb{R}$.\nDetermine whether each statement below is true or false.\n\n1. $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n2. $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in(-2,2)$.\n3. $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n4. $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $\\mathbb{R}$." :question/worked-solution "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x \\in (-2,2)$. Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/f536c645bf6b1cd0ea1bbc8576eeb69b.png)\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x \\in (-2,2)$. It consists of two smooth pieces.\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, we have\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} (-2x) = -2(-2) = 4$\nand\n$\\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} x^2 = (2)^2 = 4$.\nSo, $\\lim\\limits_{x \\to -2^+} F(x) = \\lim\\limits_{x \\to 2^-} F(x)$.\n- Statement IV is true since statements I, II, and III are all true.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/6383/Source/Images/b3c8ff64307e627d983401287be632ae.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221143 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c1e01d04-6c13-52eb-a4b6-ad9a5cde8134" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Fourier Series"}] :question/id #uuid "77c71e9d-c2a4-5a2c-9c67-a90e8913ba13" :question/math-academy-id "e-21856" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-\\dfrac{3}{4}x^2$ defined on $[MATH: [-4,4).]$ Fill in the blanks in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $8$-periodic extension of\n$G(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{4}x^3 = \\displaystyle \\int_0^x f(t) \\, \\text{d}t$\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x)$.\n\n> The Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n> $XXXX$\n> Since $f$ is\n> $xxxxxxxxxxx$\n> on $\\mathbb{R}$, we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n> $XXXX$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a_0}{2}+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a_n \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{L}\\right)\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients for\n\n$$\nn \\geq 1\n$$\n\nThus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim -4+\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{n^2\\pi^2} \\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\cos\\left(\\dfrac{n\\pi x}{4}\\right)}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4)$.\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\bbox[4pt, border: solid 0.5pt black]{\\text{piecewise continuous}}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}\\int_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{3}{4}t^{2})dt & ∼\\int_{0}^{x}(-4 + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2})\\cos (\\frac{nπt}{4}))dt \\\\ [- \\frac{1}{4}t^{3}]_{0}^{x} & ∼[- 4t]_{0}^{x} + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)(48(-1)^{n + 1})/(n^{2}π^{2}) \\cdot [\\frac{4}{nπ}\\sin (\\frac{nπt}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{4}x^{3} & ∼ - 4x + ∑_(n = 1)^(∞)192 (-1) n + 1 n 3 π 3 \\cdot sin ⁡ (n π x 4)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186221961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2ae32a2a-438f-547c-8510-7e9f760c3cb6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Properties of Periodic Extensions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9028c9b4-0eb1-5e1d-8cbb-b8155bf83fdb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220739} {:answer/id #uuid "42d01fd9-eb03-57e5-bc69-1b1db7f60d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42d01fd9-eb03-57e5-bc69-1b1db7f60d62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220740} :answer-field/id #uuid "d7a167be-384f-5c25-9282-cb9ba2153373" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220738} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9b543458-7e54-561a-9075-86139e3b8880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220742} {:answer/id #uuid "d50e4592-528d-5165-9cb2-a253443b9bed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b543458-7e54-561a-9075-86139e3b8880" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220742} :answer-field/id #uuid "5cc5726b-446b-5ee4-a6c8-b77ca10a46f0" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220741} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0adb0fd-986e-5eb1-b42e-e5710d8afacf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "True" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220745} {:answer/id #uuid "3c8a4d8e-07d8-50dd-a0c9-57ba54723b07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "False" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186220746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0adb0fd-986e-5eb1-b42e-e5710d